</tbody>
</table>

Note: grades differ because the dataset's best available stones vary. The 5ct comparison inverts because high-grade ovals are significantly rarer at that weight. For an equivalent grade comparison at 5ct, emerald cut natural diamonds run cheaper at D-VS1 than oval equivalents.

The consistent pattern from 1ct–4ct: emerald cut natural diamonds run 10–30% above oval at similar grades.

Why does the emerald cut cost more? Two reasons. First, rough diamond yield: cutting an emerald shape from rough requires removing more material per carat than cutting an oval.

Second, the emerald cut buyer has historically selected higher clarity and color grades — the market reflects that demand. You are not paying more for a better stone. You are paying more for a stone cut to the specific aesthetic demands of step-cut buyers.

